WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.662
Let's look at U N J C threads, and

2
00:00:02.762 --> 00:00:05.588
how they differ from standard U N C.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:10.252
The J means the external thread must have a controlled, rounded

4
00:00:10.352 --> 00:00:14.693
root. A rounded root spreads out stress, so the bolt resists

5
00:00:14.793 --> 00:00:19.580
fatigue cracking much better. That's why it's common in aerospace.

6
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.577
Here's a standard U N C external thread. Its root can

7
00:00:24.677 --> 00:00:29.028
be flat or sharp, and those corners are where cracks start.

8
00:00:29.132 --> 00:00:31.863
On a U N J C thread, the root is a

9
00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:37.360
required radius, roughly fifteen to eighteen percent of the pitch.

10
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.978
For the external thread, that means a larger minimum

11
00:00:41.078 --> 00:00:44.248
minor diameter, and it's held to class three A.

12
00:00:45.348 --> 00:00:49.420
Now the internal thread. A standard U N C minor diameter

13
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.816
would let the nut's crests run down into that rounded root.

14
00:00:53.916 --> 00:00:56.549
So U N J C internal threads have

15
00:00:56.649 --> 00:01:00.392
a larger minor diameter, to clear the radius.

16
00:01:01.499 --> 00:01:05.815
For the internal thread, pitch and major diameter match U N

17
00:01:05.915 --> 00:01:10.232
C class three B. The difference is the minor diameter. It's

18
00:01:10.332 --> 00:01:14.349
larger, so you drill or bore bigger, and the thread has

19
00:01:14.449 --> 00:01:19.214
a bit less engagement. Check pitch diameter with standard three B

20
00:01:19.314 --> 00:01:22.807
gauges, but check the minor diameter separately.

21
00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:26.992
Always confirm the exact limits on the print and

22
00:01:27.092 --> 00:01:29.080
in A S M E B 1 point 15.
